Question	Answer
What is a pipe?	An IPC mechanism used to transfer data between processes.
What are the types of pipes?	Named and unnamed pipes.
What is fd[0]?	The read end of the pipe.
What is fd[1]?	The write end of the pipe.
What is FIFO?	First In, First Out.
Is an ordinary POSIX pipe bidirectional?	No, it is unidirectional.
How can we achieve bidirectional communication?	By using two pipes.
What does pipe() return on success?	0.
What does fork() return in the child process?	0.
What does fork() return in the parent process?	The child process ID.
What happens when a pipe is empty?	A blocking read waits if a write end remains open.
Why should unused pipe ends be closed?	To prevent resource leaks and ensure proper EOF behaviour.
Where is pipe data stored?	In a kernel-managed buffer.
Can unrelated processes communicate using named pipes?	Yes.
